design/architecture.md:590:- Charging pads: four gold discs of 1.6 mm on the inside face of the band, 2.4 mm apart along the arc, centred opposite the block (at 158.2, 172.7, 187.3 and 201.8 degrees), set in the liner. Order VBUS, D-, D+, GND. Their place along the finger (X) is in 17.28: X = -1.0 m
design/architecture.md:604:- Cell: a start draws up to 175 mA from a cell of 20 to 27 mAh: 6.4 to 8.6 C; a running pulse below 80 mA: 2.9 to 3.9 C [CALC: 175 / 27.2, 175 / 20.4, 80 / 27.2, 80 / 20.4] (before 17.26). The cell maker must state the discharge current the cell and its protection circuit 
design/architecture.md:606:- Open: the maker's own datasheet, the price and samples (S4); the rail margin (B5); the cell's discharge limit (S12, H7).
design/architecture.md:641:- The chip limits the current drawn FROM the battery: "IBATLIM, Discharging battery current limit", 100 mA in the minimum column, "validated through simulation" (page 29 and its footnote on page 30). Nordic adds: "It has not been designed for accurate current limiting", an
design/architecture.md:710:- NOT PROVEN: nothing is measured. The cell's own discharge limit and its protection circuit's trip current stay unknown (S12): a start still takes up to 228 mA from the cell at 3.30 V with every other load on. The bench test on Nordic's kit nPM1304-EK is written in the st
design/architecture.md:711:- Also found: `design/firmware.md` line 703 sets a discharge limit that exists only on the older nPM1300; the nPM1304 has no such setting. The behaviour rules must drop it at their next revision.
